      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In standard patients, melanoma may present as a pigmented lesion that is new, changing, or asymmetric. Key characteristics include irregular borders, atypical coloration (e.g., multiple colors or shades), dotted or patchy appearance, or diameter larger than 6mm. In contrast, melanoma in patients with darker skin tones may appear less concerning due to the dense pigmentation, making it harder to detect. Look for signs of 'melasma in motion', where a melanocytic lesion appears brighter, more irregular, or has changing borders over time, rather than relying on traditional ABCDE criteria. Be cautious of sparse data in melanoma diagnosis for darker skin tones, using expertise and guidelines to identify suspicious lesions.",
